"If you could practice only one character, which one would you choose? For a very long time, calligraphy students have started with 永 (yǒng), which means forever. Its five strokes contain the eight basic ways of moving the brush in regular script. Together they are called the Eight Principles of Yong, a simple guide to the dots and lines of regular script. The eight principles have old names that sound like actions. In writing order, they are 側 for the dot, 勒 for the horizontal stroke, 努 for the vertical stroke, 趯 for the hook, 策 for the rising stroke, 掠 for the long left-falling stroke, 啄 for the short left-falling stroke, and 磔 for the right-falling stroke. 勒 is the bridle you use to rein in a horse, 策 is a horsewhip, 啄 is the way a bird pecks, and 磔 means to tear apart. But wait: 永 has only five strokes, so how can it hold eight principles? Some strokes do more than one job. The second stroke starts as a horizontal line, turns down into a vertical line, and ends with a hook. The third stroke rises to the right and then sweeps down to the left. At each turn, the brush lifts a little, presses down, and then changes direction. Each principle has its own rhythm. The dot enters, presses, and turns back. The hook stops, presses, and then flicks out quickly to a point. The left-falling strokes start heavy and become lighter and faster, while the right-falling stroke starts light, presses harder and harder, and flattens out at the end like a foot. Who invented the Eight Principles? Nobody knows for sure. Old books name different people, including Wang Xizhi, Zhong Yao, Zhiyong, and Zhang Xu. Zhiyong, a Buddhist monk who was a seventh-generation descendant of Wang Xizhi, is famous for how hard he practiced. A Tang-dynasty book says he copied the Thousand Character Classic 800 times and wore out so many brushes that he buried the old brush tips in a grave. In this lesson, watch the 3D brush write 永 one stroke at a time, and replay any of the eight principles in slow motion. Then write 永 on the practice pad in the correct stroke order: the dot, the horizontal stroke that turns down into a hook, the stroke that rises and sweeps left, the short left-falling stroke, and the right-falling stroke. The pad will compare each of your strokes with the brush's.": "65b1b095fd8c",
